5. "And it came to pass, while Zechariah was serving as priest" (the festival of the sanctification was a notable one [?]), that he put on the robe, set the plate about his head, took the incense, and walked into the inner sanctuary of the holy place. The sound of the bells ran ahead of him, and the multitude before the doors prayed, as though they had sent the priest once and for all to God as an angel and ambassador. When he was within the veil, "an angel of the Lord appeared, standing on the right of the altar of incense." Zechariah was troubled. He could not go forward, for the sight of the angel drove him back, and for a long while he stood gaping, saying to himself: "What is this that I see? I am at a loss. Someone else is serving as priest at the altar of incense; how then shall I put the incense on the fire? The law commanded that only one should serve as priest. The one I see is an angel, not a man, and this temple is not to be entered [?]; we have not been taught to minister together with angels. What will become of me? What shall I do? Whom shall I take as my counsellor? Shall I yield the holy things to the holy one? Shall I approach, greet him and hand him the censer? Will he accept me as a partner? Or has he come to call the ministers of the holy things to account? I see someone fearful and radiant. Has he come bringing good news, or threatening famine or war? That the one I see is a guard from heaven and not God, his standing shows. For Isaiah saw the Lord seated; Ezekiel beheld the Incorporeal One upon a throne; Daniel likewise saw the Ancient of Days seated. This one stands, fulfilling a ministry, and he has taken the more honourable side of the altar. Woe is me! Will he perhaps exact from me the accounts of the people as well as my own sin? Will the greatness of the honour perhaps become the occasion of a greater punishment?"

